WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.446
Network telemetry provides insight into communications between systems, services, users,

00:00:04.446 --> 00:00:05.749
and external destinations.

00:00:05.749 --> 00:00:10.964
Sources may include firewall events, DNS activity, proxy logs, network flow records,

00:00:10.964 --> 00:00:15.744
secure access systems, load balancers, and selected packet or protocol metadata.

00:00:15.744 --> 00:00:20.089
This information can reveal communication patterns, denied connections, unusual destinations,

00:00:20.089 --> 00:00:23.566
changes in volume, or unexpected relationships between assets.

00:00:23.566 --> 00:00:30.519
Network data often helps analysts connect activity across systems even when detailed endpoint events are unavailable,

00:00:30.519 --> 00:00:35.299
though encrypted traffic and changing architectures can limit what is visible.

00:00:35.299 --> 00:00:40.514
Cloud telemetry includes control-plane audit logs, identity events, workload and service logs,

00:00:40.514 --> 00:00:44.425
configuration changes, storage access, API calls, and security findings.

00:00:44.425 --> 00:00:49.205
Because cloud services are dynamic, collection should account for multiple accounts,

00:00:49.205 --> 00:00:52.246
subscriptions, regions, tenants, and service-specific logging options.

00:00:52.246 --> 00:00:57.461
Modern security programs combine on-premises and cloud telemetry to support hybrid investigations.

00:00:57.461 --> 00:01:02.676
Useful results depend on consistent identifiers, synchronized time, asset and identity context,

00:01:02.676 --> 00:01:05.718
and an understanding of which provider, customer,

00:01:05.718 --> 00:01:10.498
or service is responsible for each part of collection and retention.

00:01:10.498 --> 00:01:13.974
Shared responsibility is especially important in cloud environments.

00:01:13.974 --> 00:01:19.623
A provider may generate an audit event, while the customer must enable export,

00:01:19.623 --> 00:01:23.100
choose a destination, set retention, and control access.

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:28.314
Service onboarding should verify these responsibilities in practice so that a documented

00:01:28.314 --> 00:01:30.922
logging requirement actually results in searchable,

00:01:30.922 --> 00:01:32.660
protected, and timely evidence.
